The `as TestStatusValue` cast is unsafe and hides a potential runtime crash. `statusValueToStoryIdsMap` is only initialized with 5 keys (`pending`, `success`, `error`, `warning`, `unknown`). If a story's status has `value: 'status-value:new'` (or `modified`/`affected`) and shares a `typeId` with component tests or a11y checks — possible today via any addon that re-uses those type IDs, or if the separation between test and change-detection type IDs ever leaks — then `statusValueToStoryIdsMap['status-value:new']` will be `undefined` and `.push()` will throw at runtime. The cast tells TypeScript to trust that this never happens, but there is no guard to enforce it. Replace with a runtime check:

```typescript
const testValue = status.value as TestStatusValue;
if (testValue in statusValueToStoryIdsMap) {
  statusValueToStoryIdsMap[testValue].push(status.storyId);
}
```
